I kv: the pool backs 100% of the worst case (1 sequences all at 8192 tokens), 1.0 full-length sessions. That is concurrency at length before the scheduler preempts, not a bound on any one request. WITH PREFIX CACHING a finished sequence's blocks stay held so the next turn can reuse them, so the demand is live SESSIONS and not running requests -- when they do not all fit, the cache evicts prefixes the next turn wanted and that turn re-prefills its whole context. --host-pool-mib moves it: the experts the host pool cannot hold are the part of the card the cache cannot have.
